Q: optional controlled / uncontrolled React component with conditional React hooks A: <p>The lint error is correct: React relies on hook call order, so <code>useState</code> can't be called
conditionally. Your class version worked because it kept one <code>this.state</code> and branched
only at render time.</p>
<p>Two standard fixes:</p>
<p><strong>Option A — split into a controlled core and an uncontrolled wrapper</strong> (as the accepted
answer shows). Clean, but you now have two components to maintain, and it doesn't scale
well when a component has several optionally-controlled props.</p>
<p><strong>Option B — a hook that encapsulates the pattern</strong> (<code>useControllableState</code> from Radix or
Chakra). The hook call is unconditional; only the value/setter selection is conditional.</p>
<p>There's a third option that removes the mode branching entirely: <a href="https://github.com/wmzy/react-use-control" rel="nofollow noreferrer">react-use-control</a>(<em>disclosure: I am its author</em>).
Instead of deciding &quot;controlled or uncontrolled?&quot; every render, the component asks
&quot;was the state already created upstream?&quot; — a control object is the answer:</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>import {useControl} from 'react-use-control';

const Counter = ({count}) =&gt; {
  // unconditional; count is a control, or undefined for uncontrolled mode
  const [num, setNum] = useControl(count, 0);
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      {num.toString()}
      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setNum((n) =&gt; n + 1)}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
};

function App() {
  const [count, setCount, countCtrl] = useControl(0);
  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;Counter /&gt;                          {/* uncontrolled */}
      &lt;Counter count={countCtrl} /&gt;        {/* controlled — same code path */}
      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(0)}&gt;reset&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>One prop per state (no <code>value</code>/<code>defaultValue</code>/<code>onChange</code> triple), no <code>isControlled</code>
check, no <code>useEffect</code> sync, and no &quot;changing an uncontrolled input to controlled&quot; warning
because there is exactly one state source. The <code>initial</code> argument is ignored when a
control is passed, mirroring how React ignores <code>useState</code>'s argument on re-renders.</p>

Q: PHP: constant as variable in function A: <h2>Using Enums</h2>
<p>There is no way to evaluate constants in the way you want, short of creating something elaborate using reflection (<a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/book.reflection.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.php.net/manual/en/book.reflection.php</a>). However, starting with PHP 8.1, we finally have <em>Enums</em> (<a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/language.enumerations.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.php.net/manual/en/language.enumerations.php</a>), which can be used in cases like this. Example:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>enum Example: int
{
    case FOO = 1;
    case BAR = 2;
    case BAZ = 3;
}

function test(Example $status) {
    //function code here
}

test(1);
</code></pre>
<p>If you enter a numeric value:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>test(1);
</code></pre>
<p>It will raise a <code>TypeError</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>PHP Fatal error:  Uncaught TypeError: test(): Argument #1 ($status) must be of type Example, int given, called in test.php on line 14 and defined in test.php:10
Stack trace:
#0 test.php(14): test()
#1 {main}
  thrown in test.php on line 10
</code></pre>
<p>To adapt it to your code, you could do something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>enum ApiError: int
{
    case INVALID_REQUEST = 200;
    case UNAVAILABLE = 300;
}

class Response
{
    public function status(ApiError $status): void
    {
        ....
    }
}

$response = new Response();
$response-&gt;status(ApiError::INVALID_REQUEST);
</code></pre>
<h2>Why not SplEnum?</h2>
<p>It can certainly be used, but there are limitations:</p>
<ul>
<li>PECL is deprecated.</li>
<li>The <a href="https://pecl.php.net/package/SPL_Types" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SPL_Types</a> package may not be compatible, since the last update was in 2012.</li>
<li>You would likely need to create or use an existing <em>polyfill</em> (there are various proposals for this on GitHub).</li>
<li>You would be limited to an extended class and the constructor.</li>
</ul>

Q: Postgres plpgsql loop two times over same table result from another function A: <blockquote>
<p>Is there a way to loop twice over the same result without having to execute the query twice?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you need to modify those results, cache them in a <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createtable.html#SQL-CREATETABLE-TEMPORARY" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>temp table</code></a>:<br />
<a href="https://dbfiddle.uk/1OX0BrWD" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><sub>demo at db&lt;&gt;fiddle</sub></a></p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>create or replace function test.use_record(text) returns table(foo text) as $f$
declare rec record;
begin
  create temp table my_records as select * from test.record();
  for rec in table my_records loop
    --your pre-processing steps
    --can `insert` new, `update` or `delete` existing rows, or even `alter` the whole thing
    update my_records as target
      set foo=concat_ws(' ',target.foo,'updated')
      where target.ctid=rec.ctid;--these are implicitly created system columns
  end loop;

  delete from my_records
    where ctid=(select ctid from my_records
                order by 1 desc limit 1);--deleting one, likely most recetly modified

  return query table my_records;--return all without looping explicitly
  drop table my_records;--execution of this set-returning function doesn't end at `return` above, you can clean things up down here

end $f$ language plpgsql;
</code></pre>
<p>If you don't need to modify them and don't want to cache/materialise the first function result set, you can instead <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/plpgsql-cursors.html#PLPGSQL-CURSOR-FOR-LOOP" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>OPEN</code> a cursor</a>, loop over it, then scroll it back up and loop again. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-fetch.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>FETCH</code></a> lets you control the direction and size, absolute or relative.</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>create or replace function test.use_record(text) returns table(foo text) as $f$
declare rec record;
        cur scroll cursor for select * from test.record();
begin
  open cur;
  fetch next from cur into rec;
  while found loop
    --your pre-processing steps
    fetch next from cur into rec;
  end loop;

  fetch first in cur into rec;--scrolls it back up
  while found loop
    --your post-processing steps
    foo:=rec.foo;
    return next;
    fetch next from cur into rec;
  end loop;

  close cur;
end $f$ language plpgsql;
</code></pre>
<p>Note that the <code>scroll</code> feature of the cursor relies on your function being be <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/xfunc-volatility.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>stable</code> or <code>immutable</code></a>.</p>
<p>See if you really, really need all these loops. It could be easier and more efficient to rewrite this as plan SQL, set-based operations and hand them over for Postgres query planner to optimise.</p>
